@meta
id: 15-07
title: Any normal command, on every match
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-normal
par: 39
minutes: 6
vscode: multi-cursor on every console.log, Home, type the slashes
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
The debug prints in `OrderProvider` are wanted for one more day, so
they are not to be deleted. Comment them out instead: every
`console.log` line gets `// ` in front of it, after the indentation.
Seven of them.

In chapter 11 you did this with `I// ` on one line and `j.` on the
rest, which is fine for three lines and tedious for thirty. `:g` can run
a substitute on each line, but a substitute that has to preserve
indentation is ugly to write.

There is a third option, and it is the reason this chapter exists. The
command after the pattern does not have to be an Ex command. It can be
`normal`, followed by any sequence of ordinary normal-mode keys. Those
keys are then replayed on every marked line, as if you had moved there
and typed them yourself.

@keys
| `:g/pattern/normal keys` | on every matching line, type `keys` as if in normal mode |
| `I` | insert at the first non-blank character of the line |

@hints
The command you would type by hand on one line is `I// ` then `Esc`. Get
`:g` to type it for you.
--
After the pattern, write `normal` and a space, then the keys exactly as
you would press them. You do not need the `Esc`: when the keys run out,
vim leaves insert mode by itself.
--
`:g/console\.log/normal I// ` and Enter. Note the space after the
slashes; it is part of the text being inserted.

@solution
    :g/console\.log/normal I// <Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-two keystrokes for seven lines, and it stays thirty-two for
seventy.

`:normal` is the bridge between the two halves of vim. Everything you
have learned since chapter 1 was normal-mode keys, and `:g` was an Ex
command that could only run other Ex commands. `:g//normal` joins them:
any keystroke sequence you know becomes a per-line loop, driven by a
pattern. There is nothing to learn about the keys themselves; `I// ` is
the same `I// ` from chapter 11. What is new is the machine that types
it for you.

One mechanical detail. A `:normal` sequence that ends in insert mode is
ended for you; vim behaves as if you had pressed `Esc`. So you can leave
the `Esc` off the end. You cannot leave one out of the middle, and the
next lessons will show what to do about that.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    console.log('findOne', id);
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
    console.log('findOne result', order);

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    console.log('cancel', id);
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      console.log('cancel refused, already shipped', id);
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    console.log('refunding', order.id, order.total);
    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
    console.log('refund done', order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async markShipped(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    console.log('markShipped', order.id, order.status);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    // console.log('findOne', id);
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
    // console.log('findOne result', order);

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    // console.log('cancel', id);
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      // console.log('cancel refused, already shipped', id);
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    // console.log('refunding', order.id, order.total);
    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
    // console.log('refund done', order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async markShipped(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    // console.log('markShipped', order.id, order.status);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' },
    });
  }
}
